title: story0
---
<<declare $chance = 100>>
<b>Messenger arrives</b>: <br> A dusty messenger stumbles into your courtroom, clearly after riding many miles. Loukas Notaras, your megas doux and chief minister, takes the scroll from the messenger's shaking hand and unfolds it. You can see how the colour drains from the face of your trusted adviser. He reads it and you realise that peace has come to an end. Sultan Mehmed the Second, Lord of the Turks, Master of the Ottoman Empire, is gathering armies in the city of Adrianople. His criers are spreading the call of Jihad across Anatolia and the Balkans, his engineers are improving roads and bridges leading to Constantinople and there are rumours of massive cannons being forged. A massive army is being gathered and there can be only one target for it - the last piece of the Eastern Roman Empire, the Holy City of Constantinople, your home. Although you have suspected that this attack will come, you hoped for more time. But now, you realise that you have mere months to prepare. Notaras asks: 'Imperator, what shall be your orders? How do we prepare for this?' <br><br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[This is an extremely important decision that will shape the rest of our preparations. Your Siege Focus unlocks special choices marked with its emblem. Choose carefully: this commitment will guide the campaign.\] This focus will unlock special decisions throughout the game.</i></color> 
-> Theodosian Walls defended the City for over a thousand years, however we also need to embrace gunpowder to keep pace with the times. Our focus will be Fortifications and Artillery! #focus:0
    We will do all we can, but Fortifications and Artillery will be our main focus during the preparation for the siege. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Siege Focus is set to Fortifications and Artillery.\] This focus will unlock special decisions throughout the game.</i></color>
    <<SetInt iFocus 0>>
-> No walls can defend the City if there are no men to guard them. Our focus will be Mobilisation! #focus:1
    We will do all we can, but Mobilisation will be our main focus during the preparation for the siege. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Siege Focus is set to Mobilisation.\] This focus will unlock special decisions throughout the game.</i></color>
    <<SetInt iFocus 1>>
-> Gold is the sinews of war. We need money and resources to arm ourselves. Our focus will be War Economy! #focus:2
    We will do all we can, but War Economy will be our main focus during the preparation for the siege. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Siege Focus is set to War Economy.\] This focus will unlock special decisions throughout the game.</i></color>
    <<SetInt iFocus 2>>
-> God, not gold, will save us, as he did so many times before. If our morale falters, no weapons will help us. Our focus will be Faith and Unity! #focus:3
    We will do all we can, but Faith and Unity will be our main focus during the preparation for the siege. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Siege Focus is set to Faith and Unity.\] This focus will unlock special decisions throughout the game.</i></color>
    <<SetInt iFocus 3>>
-> We will not overcome this threat with just our swords. We need to strategise, scheme and call on our allies. Our focus will be Diplomacy and Spycraft! #focus:4
    We will do all we can, but Diplomacy and Spycraft will be our main focus during the preparation for the siege. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Siege Focus is set to Diplomacy and Spycraft.\]</i></color>
    <<SetInt iFocus 4>>

===
title: story1
---

<b>Mending the Schism</b>: <br> For years there have been attempts to mend the Great Schism between the Rome and Constantinople. Both churches proclaim themselves to be the rightful inheritors of the temple of Jesus on Earth and since 1054 Christendom has been divided between Catholics and Orthodox. Cardinal Isidore of Kiev, one of the most prominent priests of our Empire, has worked for many years to secure the so-called "Union of Florence" between Constantinople and Rome and he strongly advocates that we accept it. Not only would union with Rome heal a spiritual rift in Christianity, but it would also help us secure aid from the Pope and other Catholic nations for the upcoming siege. Meanwhile Loukas Notaras and other nobles cautiously warn you that the people and most clergy across our Empire are opposed to this. Accursed Latins have once already been the reason of our downfall, when they took the City and almost destroyed the Empire in 1204, so many can perceive this as submission on your part to the Latin Pope. We need to consider the political and religious implications. Should we stick with our tradition or consider converting to the Catholic faith, hoping to secure additional assistance?
-> We shall support Orthodox Church unto death - send Cardinal Isidore and his heretical ideas somewhere far away. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Isidore leaves permanently: unavailable as adviser or commander.\]</i></color>
    Damned be those heretics, war or peace, we shall remain Orthodox. Your decision causes a brief celebration among your elites, but you know that your decision will reduce the likelihood that Catholic nations will aid us. Cardinal Isidore is exiled to convert heathens in Lapland, he is never heard from again. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Noble Support +10\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Priest Support +15\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Religion - the Great Schism persists\]</i></color>
    <<SetInt iReligion 0>>
    <<Change fNobleRep 0.1>>
    <<Change fPriestRep 0.15>>
    <<Change bCharCardinal7 FALSE>>
-> We must seek to Mend the Schism, adopt Catholicism fully!
    The time has come to swallow our pride and admit our fault. The Emperor will convert to Catholicism and require everyone in the palace to do the same. We will fully embrace Union of Florence - Roma suprema est.  Your decision will cause anger among our priesthood and the nobles, but the Catholic nations will rejoice and we hope that the Pope shall call for a new crusade to save Constantinople. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Noble Support -20\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Priest Support -30\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Religion - the Great Schism is mended\]</i></color>
    <<SetInt iReligion 1>>
    <<Change bCharCardinal7 TRUE>>
    <<Change fNobleRep -0.2>>
    <<Change fPriestRep -0.3>>
-> The Emperor shall embrace Union of Florence, but will not enforce it. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Isidore remains available as adviser and commander.\]</i></color>
    We need the Catholic aid, but also we need to maintain our priests happy. Let us adopt Catholic faith by symbolically accepting the Union of Florence, but without enforcing it. Your decision will still somewhat annoy the priesthood and the nobility will grumble, but the Catholics will aid us, although less than they would with a full conversion. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Noble Support -5\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Priest Support -10\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Religion - the Great Schism is partially mended\]</i></color>
    <<SetInt iReligion 2>>
    <<Change bCharCardinal7 TRUE>>
    <<Change fNobleRep -0.05>>
    <<Change fPriestRep -0.1>>
-> Let us defend the City together before we settle the fate of Christendom. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Retain Orthodoxy and Isidore; clergy support -5 points; no full-Union aid benefits.\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 3>> #focus:3
    You summon Orthodox and Latin representatives to swear a wartime compact. The Empire retains its Orthodox doctrine; Latin defenders may worship according to their rites. Isidore accepts your invitation to remain as an envoy and potential commander. Some clergy resent the accommodation, and the Pope receives no promise of submission. For now, you ask both churches to defend the same walls.
    <<SetInt iReligion 0>>
    <<Change bCharCardinal7 TRUE>>
    <<Change fPriestRep -0.05>>

===

title: story2
---

<b>Siege Preparation - Walls</b>: <br> The main defences of your City are the walls separating the enemy scimitars from your throat. As everyone knows, the outer walls of the city, built by the Emperor Theodosius in the 5th century, are the greatest, most impressive piece of defensive architecture in the whole of Christendom, and possibly the whole world. The only time these walls have been breached was during the sack of the City in 1204 by the Frankish crusaders. If these walls fall, the second line of defence would be the Constantinian walls, which have been abandoned for a long time and in their current condition could not stop even a determined beggar. Finally, the core of the city is defended by the final line of Severan walls, which are also crumbling and haven't been properly maintained for centuries. In order to prepare for the siege, we need to restore our once glorious fortifications. How shall we focus on their reconstruction? <br> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Enemy will attempt to tear down your walls. When the integrity of the current wall closest to the enemy is low enough, they will assault them. After they take the outer wall, they will start attacking the next one in line. The walls are not identical and have different defence bonuses. If the enemy successfully breaches all the three walls - Theodosian, Constantinian and Severan, after the final assault you will lose the game. Do not let this happen, Empire must not be allowed to perish!\]</i></color>
-> The Theodosian Wall is the first and strongest line of defence, it needs to be repaired - this is the highest priority. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Materials -60\]</i></color> 
    Theodosian Wall is the longest and strongest of City's fortifications, stretching for 3.5 miles from the North to the South. The battle for our lives will be won or lost on its battlements, so you order its full reconstruction. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Materials -60\]</i></color>  <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Theodosian Wall will be restored to 100%\]</i></color>
    <<ChangeFraction fOuterWall 1>>   
    <<Change iMaterials -60>> 
-> The Constantinian Wall hasn't seen combat for a thousand years and is in really bad shape. However, it will be vital if the outer wall is breached. It must be rebuilt. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Materials -160\]</i></color> 
    Constantinian Wall is barely more than a ruin and for the past few centuries has mostly served as a source of bricks for the town people. However, with our efforts, it will now be restored and we will have a secondary line of defence to fall back on. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Materials -160\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Constantinian Wall will be restored to 100%\]</i></color>
    <<ChangeFraction fMiddleWall 1>>   
    <<Change iMaterials -160>> 
-> The Severan Wall is the weakest of the three, but it protects the Great Palace and the heart of the city. It must be rebuilt. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Materials -80\]</i></color> 
    The core of the city is now once more protected and Severan wall will be restored for the first time in over a millennium! While this wall is unlikely to stop a determined Turkish assault, it will definitely protect you from your own angry population or any infiltrators lurking in the city. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Materials -80\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Severan Wall will be restored to 100%\]</i></color>
    <<ChangeFraction fInnerWall 1>>   
    <<Change iMaterials -80>> 
-> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Focus - Fortifications and Artillery\]</i></color> All the three Walls must be restored before the siege begins - the siege will be long and hard. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Materials -250\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 0>> #focus:0
    You initiate the greatest reconstruction effort in Constantinople since the times of Emperor Justinian the Great. Thousands of citizens are used for the massive labour projects. Decision to repair all the Walls at once also helps to use materials more efficiently and save some of the costs. Soon, all three Walls will be restored fully - Constantinople has never been so well fortified as it is now. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Materials -250\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Theodosian Wall will be restored to 100%\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Constantinian Wall will be restored to 100%\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Severan Wall will be restored to 100%\]</i></color>
    <<ChangeFraction fOuterWall 1>> 
    <<ChangeFraction fMiddleWall 1>>  
    <<ChangeFraction fInnerWall 1>> 
    <<Change iMaterials -250>> 
===
title: story3
---
<b>Artillery Genius</b>: <br> Word reaches Constantinople that the renowned Hungarian artillery engineer, Orban, famed for his mastery in crafting devastating weaponry is arriving to offer his services to you. When the man enters your courtroom, you are surprised – he appears to be much smaller than you originally imagined him to be and his eyes wander wildly around the room. He is clearly possessed by his art – he promises to build you the greatest cannon in the world, so big it could “blast the walls of Babylon itself'. Shall we hire this mad genius to serve us?
-> This madman is too gifted to leave him wandering around, but his price is too steep. Throw him into the dungeon! <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[60% escape; 35% imprisonment; 5% fatal explosion\]</i></color>
    <<set $chance = dice(100)>>
    <<if $chance <= 60>>
    As your guards approach Orban, he screams and throws some explosive powder at the floor, which burns emitting foul smoke which blinds you and your soldiers. Mad artillery genius uses the opportunity to flee from the palace and disappear among the crowds of the Grand Market. A few weeks later your spies report that he has appeared in the Sultan’s court and has been hired to destroy your city.
    <<SetInt iGrandCannon 1>>
    <<SetInt iCourtState5 5>>
    <<elseif $chance <= 95>>
    As your guards approach Orban, he screams and attempts to resist, but Varangians break his arm and subdue him. Mad artillery genius will spend the rest of his days in Blachernae dungeons…
    <<SetInt iCourtState5 4>>
    <<else>>
    As your guards approach Orban, he screams and activates some sort of rigged grenade, triggering a massive explosion, which tears him and your soldiers into pieces. The whole throne room turns into an inferno and the blast wave throws you at the wall with the strength of a murderous giant. As you lie dying on the floor in a pool of your own blood, you think of what fate awaits your City…
    <<SetInt iCourtState5 3>>
    <<ChangeFraction fHealth -1>>
    <<endif>>
->	Hire him, whatever the cost – we need this genius on our side. Tell him to forget the Grand Cannon, but tell him to improve our artillery. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cost - 20 000 gold\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 20000 >>
    <<Change iMoney -20000>>
    Orban is pleased – he immediately gets to work on our artillery. The servants whisper among themselves that he consorts with the devils at night, for they can hear constant explosions coming from his workshop and he constantly smells of sulphur. The priesthood thinks that he is dealing with dark arts and the nobles think you went mad to invest so much money into chasing some madman’s dream when we are about to be besieged. However our artillery is improving dramatically – Orban is training new artillerymen and forging new cannons using new methods, making them more accurate and deadlier.
    <<Change fPriestRep -0.1>>
    <<Change fNobleRep -0.1>>
    <<Change bCharArtillerist5 TRUE>>
    <<Change bTechCounterCannons TRUE>>
    <<Change bTechGrapeshot TRUE>>
-><color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Focus - Fortifications and Artillery\]</i></color> Negotiate and offer him a chance to improve the artillery of the famous Theodosian walls - a task worthy of his skill. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cost - 10 000 gold\]</i></color>  <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 0 && GetInt("iMoney") >= 10000 >> #focus:0
    <<Change iMoney -10000>>
    Orban considers our offer and after some grumbling, agrees. He immediately gets to work on our artillery. The servants whisper among themselves that he consorts with the devils at night, for they can hear constant explosions coming from his workshop and he constantly smells of sulphur. The priesthood thinks that he is dealing with dark arts and the nobles think you went mad to invest so much money into chasing some madman’s dream when we are about to be besieged. However our artillery is improving dramatically – Orban is training new artillerymen and forging new cannons using new methods, making them more accurate and deadlier. 
    <<Change fPriestRep -0.1>>
    <<Change fNobleRep -0.1>>
    <<Change bCharArtillerist5 TRUE>>
    <<Change bTechCounterCannons TRUE>>
    <<Change bTechGrapeshot TRUE>>
->	Decline his offer, he is asking for too much. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[85% joins Mehmed; 15% executed by Mehmed\]</i></color>
    <<set $chance = dice(100)>>
    <<if $chance <= 85>>
    Orban curses and departs your throne room in anger. A few weeks later your spies report that he has appeared in the Sultan’s court and has been hired to destroy your city.
    <<SetInt iGrandCannon 1>>
    <<SetInt iCourtState5 5>>
    <<else>>
    Orban curses and departs your throne room in anger. A few weeks later your spies report that he has tried to sell his services to the Sultan, but Mehmed has chosen instead to cut his head off.
    <<SetInt iCourtState5 3>>
    <<endif>>
===
title: story4
---
<b>Hungry Mouths</b>: <br> As the rumours about the coming Turkish invasion spread, people from the countryside start trickling into the City. At first they come in small numbers, a family here or a merchant there, but soon whole villages start flocking towards the mirage of safety in the Capital. Loukas Notaras, your Mesazon, chief minister, comes to you with concern in his eyes, arguing that a decision needs to be taken as to what course of action should be taken regarding these refugees. As the Emperor, you have to consider a few factors. These are your subjects and as many of them as possible should be saved. However, Constantinople already houses {GetPopulation()} people, and all of them need to eat. Once the siege begins, the stores must feed everyone sheltered within the walls. Additional people will mean higher food consumption, but greater population will also provide more taxes, recruits and manpower for labour. This is one of the most important decisions to take before the siege.
<color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[City's food supplies and basic necessities, or Provisions, are consumed by the population daily. Every 1,000 residents consume 1 unit of Provisions per day after the siege begins. For example, 87,000 residents consume 87 units of Provisions each day. When provisions run out, hunger brings urgent appeals to the council. Buy supplies, requisition private stores or bear the loss of morale. Provisions cost 2 gold each during preparations and 10 during the siege. Food planning is an essential part of siege strategy. However Population is not merely a burden, it also provides your recruitment pool, taxation base and can play an important role in some events. Also, these are your subjects and as their Imperator, you swore to protect them in front of the Almighty himself. Good luck!\]</i></color>
-> The City is the final bastion of the Roman civilisation and everyone is welcome, open the gates!
    <<RndChange iPopulation 20000 50000>>
    <<Change fPeopleRep 0.3>> 
    <<Change fNobleRep 0.05>> 
    <<Change fTroopRep 0.1>> 
    <<Change fPriestRep 0.1>>  
    Thousands upon thousands of people flock through the gate of St.Romanus. You come to inspect the crowds yourself and thousands of cheering, happy faces greet you. Everyone, even the nobles, feels that this is the right thing to do. For the time being you feel that you are doing your duty as the saviour of your people. What effect it will have, only time will tell. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Noble Support +5\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Priest Support +10\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Troop Morale +10\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Public Morale +30\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[City Population is now {GetPopulation()} \]</i></color>
-> We need to save the flower of the nation, but leave the peasants beyond the gate. Tax those who enter - everyone needs to contribute to the defence!
    <<RndChange iPopulation 5000 10000>>
    <<Change fPeopleRep -0.1>>
    <<Change fNobleRep -0.1>> 
    <<Change fTroopRep -0.1>> 
    <<Change fPriestRep -0.1>> 
    <<RndChange iMoney 10000 20000>>
    You open the gates to the nobles and their servants, priests, merchants, craftsmen and tax all of them on entry to help pay for the siege costs. Your decision is rational, but depressing. No one seriously objects, but there is a feeling of gloom around the city. Everyone is thinking - what if I had ended up on the other side of the gate? <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Noble Support -10\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Priest Support -10\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Troop Morale -10\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Public Morale -10\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[City Population is now {GetPopulation()} \]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Your treasury is now {GetInt("iMoney")} \]</i></color>
-> Close the gate, we are full. No one is to be admitted inside - we will be barely able to feed the population we already have. 
    The wailing of the refugees beyond the gate doesn't let you sleep for a few days, but soon, they realise that nothing will change and start departing. You may have turned the hungry mouths away, but most of the City sees you as a cruel ruler who betrayed his own people. Only time will tell if you were right to do this. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Noble Support -5\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Priest Support -15\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Troop Morale -15\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Public Morale -30\]</i></color>
    <<Change fPeopleRep -0.3>>
    <<Change fNobleRep -0.05>> 
    <<Change fTroopRep -0.15>> 
    <<Change fPriestRep -0.15>> 
-> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Focus - Mobilisation\]</i></color> Allow everyone in, but press all the able bodied refugees to serve in our armed forces. Send the peasants to train with the spears and the hunters shall become archers. <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 1>> #focus:1
    Their quality is poor, but these men know that they owe their lives to you, they shall fight like beasts when the Turks come, so the morale of your army improves. You gain 2000 infantry and 500 archers. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Public Morale +20\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Troop Morale +10\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Infantry +2000\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Archers +500\]</i></color> 
    <<Change fPeopleRep 0.2>>
    <<RndChange iPopulation 20000 40000>> //PROBLEM: post-choice display
    <<Change iInfantry 2000>>
    <<Change iArchers 500>>
    <<Change fTroopRep 0.1>> 
    <<Change fInfantryQuality -0.2>> //PROBLEM: quality steps - REDUCTION
    <<Change fArchersQuality -0.2>> //PROBLEM: quality steps - REDUCTION
-> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Focus - Diplomacy and Spycraft\]</i></color> Order our agents to divert as many refugees away from the city by spreading rumours. <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 4 && GetInt("iMoney") >= 2500>> #focus:4
    The plan works, our agents manage to successfully divert the majority of the refugees away from Constantinople by spreading rumours among the refugees about the awful conditions in the City, while mentioning that Peloponnese is very nice this time of year and not as far as it seems, so they should consider going there instead. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Money -2500\]</i></color>
    <<RndChange iPopulation 3000 7000>> //PROBLEM: post-choice display
    <<Change iMoney -2500>> 
-> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Focus - Fortifications and Artillery\]</i></color> Let us turn this mass of people into a labour force to gather materials to better prepare for the siege. <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 0>> #focus:0
    <<RndChange iPopulation 15000 30000>> //PROBLEM: post-choice display
    <<RndChange iMaterials 600 1200>> //PROBLEM: post-choice display
    <<Change fPeopleRep -0.1>>
    Your taskmasters soon organise labour camps outside of the gate of St. Romanos where the refugees are used to cut down timber from the neighbouring Veligrádio forest. Anyone who wants to enter through the gate, must also bring some timber or stone from the local quarry. Not everyone is physically fit enough to participate in this labour and many thousands of refugees instead choose to depart elsewhere. The people feel that this is a harsh decision, but nobles, priests and soldiers understand that this is a necessary measure for the safety of the City. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Public Morale -10\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Materials are now {GetInt("iMaterials")} \]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[City Population is now {GetPopulation()} \]</i></color>   
===
title: story5
---
<b>The Genoese Arrive </b>: <br> As the ominous shadow of the impending Ottoman siege looms over Constantinople, a timely reinforcement sails into the Golden Horn from the maritime republic of Genoa, led by the seasoned commander Giovanni Giustiniani. This contingent offers 700 soldiers: 500 infantry and 200 archers. They join your army if you accept their service; turning them away adds no troops. The potential impact of this Genoese contingent on the city's defence cannot be overstated. However, you are aware that Giustiniani is a cunning warlord, who is motivated by a chance for glory and riches. How shall we greet him?
-> We need every blade we can get, let us promise him the isle of Lemnos if he manages to help us defend our city!
    You promise Giustiniani the isle of Lemnos if the City survives. He accepts before the wine is poured. His men unload their arms and march to the walls. Your nobles mutter that the Empire is being sold piecemeal to mercenaries.
    <<Change fNobleRep -0.1>>
    <<Change bCharWarrior0 1>> 
    <<Change iInfantry 500>>
    <<Change iArchers 200>>
-> Turn them away, Giustiniani is too ambitious, we cannot trust him!
    <<Change bCharWarrior0 FALSE>>
    You make a decision that additional support from the Genoese is not worth the risk of them essentially taking over your City. Your decision strengthens your position among the nobility and priests, but Galatan podestà is not pleased...
    <<Change fPriestRep 0.1>>
    <<Change fNobleRep 0.1>>
    <<Change fGalataRep -0.1>>
-> While Giustiniani will not be easy to control, we do need his troops... Poison him. <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 4 && GetInt("iSpies") >= 1 >> #focus:4
    <<Change bCharWarrior0 FALSE>>
    <<SetInt iCourtState0 3>>
    Lord Giovanni is greeted with a great feast. Unfortunately, over the following weeks he begins to feel increasingly sick, until he unfortunately dies. His men soon discover and kill a spy among his staff that was secretly putting poison into his food. Accursed Turks must have felt the danger from this noble man! His troops are now taken over by Francesco, his second-in-command, a skilled commander, but a commoner with much more manageable ambitions.
    <<Change iSpies -1>> 
    <<Change iInfantry 500>>
    <<Change iArchers 200>>
===
title: story6
---
<b>Siege Preparation — Provisions Planning</b>: <br> One of the most important things to survive the siege will be to have adequate provisions to feed the population of the City. We need to take urgent measures to hoard enough food for everyone.  However, food costs money and merchants are likely to start raising prices the more we buy, as it becomes scarcer. How much food should we procure and how?
-> We have enough food and this siege won't take longer than a month - there is no need to waste money or worry people.
    Our confidence inspires the people, but our advisers think that we are mad.
    <<Change fPeopleRep 0.1>>
-> Buy all the food in the city. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cost - 6000 gold\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 6000>>
    The head of the palace stores goes around all of the merchants in the city and bought everything available on the market.
    <<Change iProvisions 3000>> 
    <<Change iMoney -6000>> 
-> Buy all the food in the province.<color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cost - 15 000 gold\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 15000>>
    Merchants are quick to notice that there's a chance to make a killing and they raise the prices, but we still manage to secure a significant supply of food.
    <<Change iProvisions 6000>> 
    <<Change iMoney -15000>> 
-> Buy the food from everyone, even the Turkish smugglers from across the Straits. It will cost an arm and a leg, but it is necessary. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cost - 35 000 gold\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 35000>>
    <<Change iProvisions 10000>> 
    <<Change iMoney -35000>> 
    Desperate times call for desperate measures. We have spent so much money that our treasurer is crying, but at least our warehouses are absolutely brimming with food.
-> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Focus - War Economy\]</i></color> We could implement absolute food control in all of our domains. Our troops will confiscate all the food and then ration it. This will be highly unpopular and implementing this will still cost money, but we will secure a massive supply of food. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cost - 10 000 gold & Reputation loss\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 2 && GetInt("iMoney") >= 10000>> #focus:2
    <<Change iProvisions 20000>> 
    <<Change iMoney -10000>> 
    <<Change fPeopleRep -0.1>>
    <<Change fNobleRep -0.1>> 
    <<Change fTroopRep -0.1>> 
    <<Change fPriestRep -0.1>> 
    With an iron fist Imperator has declared that food in your mouth now belongs to the Empire and you will eat only when you are ordered and only how much you are ordered. Everyone is unhappy, but everyone will have something to eat.
===
title: story7
---
<<Change bCharDiplomat4 TRUE>>
<b>Siege Preparation — Lord of the Imperial Wardrobe</b>: <br> George Sphrantzes brings two inventories. One records the robes, silver vessels and embroidered hangings of your household. The other records what buyers will pay for them.<br>He has carried your letters through foreign courts and knows what an Emperor's appearance can purchase. He also knows what soldiers cost. “We can keep the silver on the table,” he says, “or keep men beside the gates. I would prefer both. The accounts are less accommodating.”<br>The nobles warn that an emptied palace will proclaim imperial poverty to every visiting envoy. Sphrantzes lays the inventories beside one another and waits. He is available in your court as a diplomatic adviser.
<<set $chance = dice(100)>>
<<if $chance <= 50>>
A merchant offers 6,000 gold for the collection. Buyers are still willing to gamble on the City's survival.
<<else>>
The approaching war has frightened the buyers. The best offer is only 4,000 gold.
<<endif>>
-> Sell the collection. A splendid court is no use in a fallen City. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Gold +4,000 or +6,000; noble support -5 points.\]</i></color>
    <<if $chance <= 50>>
    <<Change iMoney 6000>>
    <<else>>
    <<Change iMoney 4000>>
    <<endif>>
    <<Change fNobleRep -0.05>>
    The silver leaves the palace under guard. Its price is counted into the war treasury; the nobles will remember what you sold.
-> Preserve the collection. The imperial household must continue to function.
    Sphrantzes seals the stores again. The court will receive its envoys beneath the old hangings, and find the money elsewhere.

===
title: story8
---
<b>Siege Preparation — Call on a Major Christian Power</b>: <br> Alone we shall fall, that is certain to everyone from megas doux himself down to the blind Cuman beggar wailing by the walls of the Hippodrome. But not all is lost. The time has come to call upon Christendom and ask for their help. Recent battle of Varna in 1444 has been a major defeat for the Christian nations, who suffered heavy casualties against the Ottomans. The memories are still fresh, but maybe we could offer them a chance to even the score? Whom shall we reach out to in the first place?
-> Send our envoy to the Pope. As the leader of Christendom, he is guaranteed to provide at least some aid. <<if GetInt("iReligion") == 2 || GetInt("iReligion") == 1>> 
    <<set $chance = dice(100)>>
    <<if $chance <= 40>> 
    The Pope sends 6 ships packed with food, carrying money and 600 archers, along with his letter in which he offers his prayers for the soul of Constantinople and a promise of further aid.
    <<Change iProvisions 3000>>
    <<Change iMoney 10000>> 
    <<Change iArchers 600>>
    <<Change iShips 6>>
    <<else>>
    The Pope sends 2 ships packed with food, carrying money and 200 archers, along with his letter in which he offers his prayers for the soul of Constantinople.
    <<Change iProvisions 1000>>
    <<Change iMoney 10000>> 
    <<Change iArchers 200>>
    <<Change iShips 2>> 
    <<endif>>
-> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Focus - Diplomacy and Spycraft\]</i></color> As we have accepted Catholicism fully, send our envoy to the Pope and request him to launch a new crusade to help us! <<if GetInt("iReligion") == 1 && GetInt("iFocus") == 4>>  #focus:4
    <<set $chance = dice(100)>>
    <<if $chance <= 30>> 
    Tireless work of your diplomats and the wise decision to mend the Schism create the conditions which allow the Pope to call the Xth crusade, this time to protect Constantinople and repay the sins of the IVth crusade, when Western crusaders sacked the City! Soon, pilgrim warriors flock to your cause, Italians, French, English, Scottish, Spanish, Germans, Portuguese, Hungarians, Polish, Lithuanians, Danes and even a few Swedes. Over the coming months some 3000 infantry, 4000 archers and 800 cavalry arrive, along with maybe 15 000 pilgrims and camp followers who join your general population. The morale of your defenders has never been higher!
    <<Change iInfantry 3000>>
    <<Change iArchers 4000>>
    <<Change iCavalry 800>>
    <<Change fTroopRep 0.4>>
    <<Change iPopulation 15000>>
    <<elseif $chance <= 80>> 
    While the Christian kingdoms are not yet ready to commit to a full crusade, many send their volunteers to help you. Venetian and Genoese mercenaries also soon arrive, bringing 600 infantry and 600 archers to support the defence of your city.
    <<Change iInfantry 600>>
    <<Change iArchers 600>>
    <<else>>
    Memories of the defeat at Varna are too fresh... Your call for a new crusade falls on deaf ears, but you do receive a lot of supportive thoughts and prayers directed your way. Some even send you some money, but it feels like a disappointment...
    <<RndChange iMoney 1000 5000>>
    <<endif>>
-> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Focus - Faith and Unity\]</i></color> As we have embraced the truth of Orthodoxy and rejected Roman heresy, we send our envoys to Trebizond and the Georgian Kingdom, stressing that if Constantinople falls, it will be the end of the Orthodox world and that they will be next. Announce the first Orthodox Crusade to save Constantinople and call all righteous Stavrophoroi - Orthodox crusaders - to our banner! <<if GetInt("iReligion") == 0 && GetInt("iFocus") == 3>>  #focus:3
    <<set $chance = dice(100)>>
    <<if $chance <= 20>> 
    In our days, Orthodox realms are few and weak, but your call raises their hearts and triggers an unexpected movement of support for your cause. Orthodox monks from the villages of Ruthenia to the mountains of Armenia ring bells and preach the need to defend Constantinople. Soon, pilgrim warriors flock to your cause, Bulgarians, Georgians, Armenians, Serbs, Ruthenians and even a few Cossacks. Over the coming months some 8000 infantry, 2500 archers and 1200 cavalry arrive, along with maybe 15 000 pilgrims and camp followers who join your general population.
    <<Change iInfantry 8000>>
    <<Change iArchers 2500>>
    <<Change iCavalry 1200>>
    <<Change fTroopRep 0.5>>
    <<Change iPopulation 15000>>
    <<elseif $chance <= 80>> 
    In our days, Orthodox realms are few and weak, and there is no will among them for a crusade, but some nonetheless answer the call. Georgian nobility sends 100 young knights and soon a ship of volunteers from Ruthenia arrives, adding another 400 infantry. Also 200 Armenian archers join your cause as well.
    <<Change iInfantry 400>>
    <<Change iArchers 200>>
    <<Change iCavalry 100>>
    <<Change iShips 1>>
    <<else>>
    In our days, Orthodox realms are few and weak. They close their eyes and ears to your pleas.
    <<endif>>
-> Make a general call for help to all the Christian realms. You know that your success here will depend greatly on how you managed to mend the Schism...
    <<if GetInt("iReligion") == 0>>
    In our days, Orthodox realms are few and weak, but they answer the call. Georgian nobility sends 50 young knights and soon a ship of volunteers from Ruthenia arrives, adding another 200 infantry.
    <<Change iInfantry 200>>
    <<Change iCavalry 50>>
    <<Change iShips 1>>
    <<elseif GetInt("iReligion") == 1>>
    Your recent conversion to Catholicism has moved the hearts of believers. Knights Hospitaller have sent their fleet from Rhodes to support you, bringing 100 knights, 200 archers, 200 infantry and 5 ships.
    <<Change iInfantry 200>>
    <<Change iArchers 200>>
    <<Change iCavalry 100>>
    <<Change iShips 5>> 
    <<elseif GetInt("iReligion") == 2>>
    While you are not enforcing Union of Florence, pragmatically minded Italians nonetheless see an opportunity to find a place for themselves in your new Empire. Venetian and Genoese mercenaries soon arrive, bringing 400 infantry and 400 archers to support the defence of your city.
    <<Change iInfantry 400>>
    <<Change iArchers 400>>
    <<endif>>
===
title: story9
---
<b>Siege Preparation — Mind over Matter</b>: <br> Johannes Grant arrives with dust on his boots and a drawing of a wall's foundations. He has spent the morning examining a stretch that your officers consider sound.<br>“Above ground, perhaps,” he says. He places a bowl on the table and taps the wood beneath it. The water trembles. “A man with a shovel must make a noise. Give me men who know how to listen.”<br>He asks for 3,000 gold to equip a workshop and train ten engineers. Repairs, countermines and the unseen weaknesses of stone will become his charge.
-> Hire Grant and equip his workshop. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Gold -3,000; engineers +10; Grant available.\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 3000>>
    <<Change iMoney -3000>>
    <<Change iEngineers 10>>
    <<Change bCharArchitect6 TRUE>>
    Grant establishes his workshop and begins training your engineers.
    <<set $chance = dice(100)>>
    <<if $chance <= 40>>
    A survey uncovers a forgotten stock of sound timber and dressed stone. Your engineers recover 40 materials for the defence.
    <<Change iMaterials 40>>
    <<endif>>
-> Rely on our existing engineers. We cannot promise more money.
    <<Change bCharArchitect6 FALSE>>
    Grant leaves without a commission. Your existing engineering staff remains unchanged.

===
title: story10
---
<b>Siege Preparation — Recruitment</b>: <br> The people of Constantinople have grown used to peace and our military has for a long time been underfunded and the troops we have are too weak to withstand Ottoman might. The answer is clear - we need more men. There are many ways to recruit more soldiers, but which one is the best?
-> Infantry is the backbone of our army, they will hold the walls during the enemy assaults. Raise 3000 infantry.<color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cost - 30 000 gold\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 30000>>
    We quickly recruit more spearmen from the citizens of Constantinople. Some have never held a weapon in their lives. They will learn quickly or they will die. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Money -30000\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Infantry +3000\]</i></color> 
    <<Change iInfantry 3000>>
    <<Change iMoney -30000>>
-> Nothing is more dangerous when approaching the walls than a well-aimed arrow. Ranged troops are most effective at killing attackers - raise 1500 archers.<color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cost - 30 000 gold\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 30000>>
    You order the ancient Hippodrome to be converted into three archery ranges, where hundreds of archers train daily. By the time the siege comes, they should be ready. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Money -30000\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Archers +1500\]</i></color> 
    <<Change iArchers 1500>>
    <<Change iMoney -30000>>
-> Cavalry allows us to sally out and strike at our enemy directly, but also the knights can dismount and fight on the walls, so let us raise 300 cavalry.<color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cost - 30 000 gold\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 30000>>
    One does not become a knight overnight, but you hurry to ennoble promising candidates. This new force will train day and night to prepare for the war. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Money -30000\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cavalry +300\]</i></color> 
    <<Change iCavalry 300>>
    <<Change iMoney -30000>>
-> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Focus - Mobilisation\]</i></color> Return to tradition and raise a Legion of 5000 infantry and 1000 archers, in honour of our Roman ancestors. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cost - 50 000 gold\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 1 && GetInt("iMoney") >= 50000 >> #focus:1
    On your orders, the whole city is turned into a massive training encampment. As per ancient tradition, a legionary Aquila is forged from gold, perched atop an Orthodox Cross, joining the Roman heritage with our Christian faith. Freshly minted legionaries are motivated, but only time will tell if they deserve the name of their forebears. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Money -50000\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Infantry +5000\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Archers +1000\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Troop Morale +10\]</i></color>
    <<Change iInfantry 5000>>
    <<Change iArchers 1000>>
    <<Change fTroopRep 0.1>> 
    <<Change iMoney -50000>>
-> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Focus - Faith and Unity\]</i></color> We call upon the religious fanatics, forming their mobs into military units. They will be poorly trained, but cheap to raise. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cost - 3000 gold\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 3 && GetInt("iMoney") >= 3000 >> #focus:3
    While the nobles don't like our decision, these new fanatical troops are very motivated and numerous, numbering around 3000. But the best thing about them is that they are cheap - all we need to do is to pay for their weapons. However, they are not trained, don't like taking orders from your officers and the overall quality of your infantry suffers. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Money -3000\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Infantry +3000\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Noble Support -5\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Troop Morale +30\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Infantry Quality is decreased\]</i></color> 
    <<Change iInfantry 3000>>
    <<Change fNobleRep -0.05>> 
    <<Change fTroopRep 0.3>>  
    <<Change fInfantryQuality -0.2>> //PROBLEM: quality steps
    <<Change iMoney -3000>>
-> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Focus - Diplomacy and Spycraft\]</i></color> Summon foreign mercenaries, promising them land and titles after the war if we are victorious. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Loss of support\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 4>> #focus:4
    Soon brigands and mercenaries of all kinds flock to our banner. Primarily they are Italians from various states, but Cumans also come, Bulgars and even some Turks. They are not very numerous, but they are hardened killers, veterans of many wars, so their presence raises the quality of your troops across the board. However, their presence, unfamiliar tongues and strange customs unnerve your people, priests, nobles and even other troops. In total, 750 infantry, 750 archers and 100 cavalry join your ranks. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Infantry +750\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Archers +750\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cavalry +100\]</i></color>  <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Noble Support -5\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Priest Support -5\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Troop Morale -5\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Public Morale -5\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Infantry Quality is increased\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Archers Quality is increased\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cavalry Quality is increased\]</i></color> 
    <<Change iInfantry 750>>
    <<Change iArchers 750>>
    <<Change iCavalry 100>>
    <<Change fNobleRep -0.05>> 
    <<Change fTroopRep -0.05>> 
    <<Change fPriestRep -0.05>> 
    <<Change fPeopleRep -0.05>> 
    <<Change fInfantryQuality 0.2>> //PROBLEM: quality steps - Increase
    <<Change fArchersQuality 0.2>> //PROBLEM: quality steps - Increase
    <<Change fCavalryQuality 0.2>> //PROBLEM: quality steps - Increase
-> Postpone the levy. We must defend the City with the men we can afford.
    The muster rolls are closed for now. No new troops are raised; the existing garrison will have to carry the burden.

===
title: story11
---
<b>Siege Preparation — Morean Orders</b>:  <br> While the territories under the control of the Eastern Roman Empire have waxed and waned over the centuries, never have they been smaller than today. Grandeur that was once Rome is lost and buried. Constantinople is the only major city left under the control of our Empire, however we still have holdings in the Morean peninsula (also known as Peloponnesus) in southern Greece and in some of the islands of the Marmara sea. As the siege approaches, we need to issue orders to our troops serving there. We could summon them to help us, but it could anger our nobles, whose estates would remain unprotected. Alternatively, we could tell them to launch a diversion against the Turks and hopefully distract some of their troops. What shall be your orders?
-> They should remain where they are and protect our lands from bandits and raiders, support of the nobility will be important to me.
    The nobles are pleased with your decision and promise to make good use of the imperial troops to protect your lands. They also send you a monetary contribution worth 15000 gold to support siege preparation. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Money +15000\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Noble Support +5\]</i></color>
    <<Change iMoney 15000>> 
    <<Change fNobleRep 0.05>> 
-> Summon all of the trained troops to return to Constantinople. If the City falls, nothing else will matter, we need every bow and every blade on our holy walls!
    Soon, a few ships from Morea arrive, bringing the whole garrison - a total of 1000 soldiers, 400 archers and 100 cavalry. The people of the City rejoice, as this significantly bolsters your forces. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Infantry +1000\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Archers +400\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cavalry +100\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Noble Support -10\]</i></color>
    <<Change iInfantry 1000>> 
    <<Change iArchers 400>> 
    <<Change iCavalry 100>> 
    <<Change fNobleRep -0.1>>  
-> Order the lords of Morea to distract Mehmed's forces by attacking his supply lines, this could become an important factor in the later stages of the siege.
    While the distraction is too small to truly disrupt the main Ottoman army, Sultan Mehmed nonetheless sends 12 500 soldiers to hunt down your troops. This means that they won't participate in the siege, however the nobility think that your orders are a suicide mission, hurting your image. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Enemy Troops 'Bashi-Bazouk Irregulars' is reduced by -12500\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Noble Support -15\]</i></color>
    <<Change iBazouks -12500>> 
    <<Change fNobleRep -0.15>>
    <<Change bMoreanDistraction>>
-> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Focus - Mobilisation\]</i></color> We should order every able bodied man from our holdings to come to defend Constantinople immediately. <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 1>> #focus:1
    While the majority of the recruits are Morean farmers without much fighting experience, their numbers will significantly bolster our fighting strength. Unfortunately, the mood among soldiers turns sour as they mix with country bumpkins. Soon, a few ships arrive, bringing 3000 soldiers, 500 archers and 100 cavalry.  <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Infantry +3000\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Archers +500\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cavalry +100\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Troop Morale -7\]</i></color>
    <<Change iInfantry 3000>> 
    <<Change iArchers 500>> 
    <<Change iCavalry 100>> 
    <<Change fTroopRep -0.07>>  
-> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Focus - War Economy\]</i></color> We should instead demand that our holdings provide all the resources and money they can. <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 2>> #focus:2
    The troops, instead of travelling to Constantinople, are used to shake everyone dry, collecting every penny for your treasury. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Noble Support -7\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Priest Support -7\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Public Morale -7\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Money +25000\]</i></color>
    <<Change iMoney 25000>> 
    <<Change fNobleRep -0.07>>  
    <<Change fPriestRep -0.07>>
    <<Change fPeopleRep -0.07>>    
->  <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Focus - Faith and Unity\]</i></color> We should instead order all of our holdings to pray for us. This will raise morale of all the defenders. <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 3>> #focus:3
    Every church in Morea is ringing bells day and night, every soldier and peasant spends their days praying for their Imperator and Constantinople. This raises the mood across the board, as people, soldiers, priests and even the nobles are astounded by such a show of piety. God will truly be on our side now! <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Noble Support +12\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Priest Support +12\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Troop Morale +12\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Public Morale +12\]</i></color>
    <<Change fNobleRep 0.12>>  
    <<Change fPriestRep 0.12>>
    <<Change fPeopleRep 0.12>>
    <<Change fTroopRep 0.12>>   

===
title: story12
---
<b>Siege Preparation — Should We Trust the Venetians?</b>: <br> Alvise Diedo stands beneath mosaics that survived the sack of 1204. The council needs no account of what the crusaders carried away, nor of the Venetian ships that brought them. The wound has passed from fathers to sons; even men who were not born into the ruin know its shape.<br>Yet the harbour needs captains, and Diedo has brought an offer rather than an apology: two ships, two hundred armed volunteers, wages and provisions guaranteed by the Emperor. “Judge the men who stand before you,” he says. “There will be time enough to judge the dead.”<br>Accepting his service will strengthen the harbour. It will also put Venetian officers beside men whose families still count Venice among the City's enemies.
-> Guarantee their service. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Gold -2,500; ships +2; infantry +200; Diedo available.\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 2500>>
    <<Change iMoney -2500>>
    <<Change iShips 2>>
    <<Change iInfantry 200>>
    <<Change bCharAdmiral1 TRUE>>
    Diedo accepts the agreement. His ships enter your naval service, his volunteers join the garrison, and he becomes available as a commander.
-> Receive Diedo as an adviser, without hiring his contingent.
    <<Change bCharAdmiral1 TRUE>>
    Diedo accepts a place at your council. His ships remain outside the imperial muster, but his knowledge of the harbour is now at your disposal. He will have to earn the trust of the men beside him.
-> Decline the offer. We will preserve the treasury.
    <<Change bCharAdmiral1 FALSE>>
    Diedo departs with his contingent. No ships or troops are added to your forces.

===
title: story13
---
<b>Siege Preparation — Our Turks</b>: <br> The men outside your door are Turks, but they have come from your own streets and estates. Some draw imperial pay; others serve lords who owe you obedience. Their wives buy bread in our markets. Their children speak Greek beside Turkish. Now the Sultan is coming, and every familiar face invites an unfamiliar suspicion.<br>Orhan speaks for the soldiers. “Mehmed has a throne for himself and a grave for me. Ask which of us has more reason to hold your walls.”<br>The council cannot promise that every household shares his predicament. Traders still have cousins across the straits. A messenger needs neither a uniform nor a sword.
-> Exile the Turkish households and dismiss their retainers.
    <<TurkishHouseholds 1>>
    Families pass through the gates under escort, carrying what the carts will bear. Some leave silently; others demand to know which oath they have broken. Orhan departs without a command. Their service is lost to us.
-> Keep them under close supervision. Admit a limited contingent under Roman officers. \[Infantry +150; Orhan held under guard.\]
    <<TurkishHouseholds 2>>
    The captains surrender their lists of men and weapons. Their soldiers are distributed among Roman companies, their movements watched. Orhan remains under guard. We have gained men, though few friends.
-> Confirm their privileges and honour their service. Let Orhan command them. \[Infantry +300; noble support −5 points; Orhan available.\]
    <<TurkishHouseholds 3>>
    You receive their oath before the court and confirm their households under imperial protection. Orhan kneels, then rises to receive his commission. Among the nobles, several faces remain carefully expressionless. On the muster ground, three hundred men answer the call.
===
title: story14
---
<b>Siege Preparation — Commander</b>: <br> The council awaits your choice. Your commander will direct the defence and receive the stronger command benefit shown below.
-> Appoint Giustiniani. Infantry lethality +15%. <<if GetBool("bCharWarrior0")>>
    Giustiniani takes command. His captains begin drilling the infantry for the fighting at the breaches.
    <<SetInt iCommander 0>>
-> Appoint Orhan. Enemy casualties in assaults +15%. <<if GetBool("bCharNomad3")>>
    Orhan studies the Ottoman formations and prepares the garrison to exploit their weak points.
    <<SetInt iCommander 3>>
-> Appoint Orban. Cannon lethality +20%. <<if GetBool("bCharArtillerist5")>>
    Orban sends for the master gunners. Each battery receives its targets and its share of powder.
    <<SetInt iCommander 5>>
-> Appoint Notaras. Wall repair gold costs -15%. <<if GetBool("bCharAdministrator2")>>
    Notaras calls for the accounts, the storehouse keys and the names of every foreman on the walls.
    <<SetInt iCommander 2>>
-> Appoint Isidore. Effective army morale in breach tests +10 points. <<if GetBool("bCharCardinal7")>>
    Isidore visits the garrison before taking his seat at the council. He asks the chaplains to accompany their men on the walls.
    <<SetInt iCommander 7>>
-> Appoint Diedo. Ranged lethality +15% while the fleet defends the harbour. <<if GetBool("bCharAdmiral1")>>
    Diedo calls the captains together. Signals and positions are agreed before the ships return to their stations.
    <<SetInt iCommander 1>>
-> Appoint Grant. Wall defence +6 points. <<if GetBool("bCharArchitect6")>>
    Grant takes his officers around the circuit of the walls, stopping at every weak point to assign men and tools.
    <<SetInt iCommander 6>>
-> Appoint Sphrantzes. Aid acceptance +20 points. <<if GetBool("bCharDiplomat4")>>
    Sphrantzes puts the envoys to work. Every appeal now carries the authority of the commander of the defence.
    <<SetInt iCommander 4>>
-> Appoint {VisitorName()}. {VisitorAbility()} <<if VisitorInService()>>
    Your invited adviser receives the commander's seal. The officers gather to hear the new dispositions for the defence.
    <<AppointVisitor>>
-> Keep the defence under my personal direction for now.
    <<SetInt iCommander -1>>
    You postpone the appointment. The officers retain their posts, but the defence receives no appointed commander bonus.

===
title: story15
---
<b>Siege Preparation — Galata Decision</b>: <br> Galata, also known as Pera, holds a complicated place in the history of our City. Your ancestor, Emperor Michael VIII Palaiologos, granted it to the Genoese in 1267 in accordance with the Treaty of Nymphaeum. They were prohibited from fortifying it, but the Latins, as is their habit, disregarded this and, through subsequent expansions of the walls, enlarged the area of their settlement. Our history with the Genoese is complicated – on the one hand, they are Christians, fine soldiers, and merchants. Their trade makes our City prosperous, and a significant part of our fleet is crewed by Genoese mercenary captains and sailors. <br> On the other hand, coin is their highest master, and they have their own ambitions to enrich themselves and build their own trade Empire around the Mediterranean and the Black Sea. Pragmatic to the core, they have even occasionally supported the Ottomans – for example, before the recent crusade of Varna in 1444, when Genoese ships transported Sultan Murad’s army across the Bosporus in exchange for one gold piece per soldier. This led to one of the greatest disasters of recent times, as the Christian armies were defeated, emboldening the Turks and exposing Constantinople to the current threat. <br> Today, the Genoese of Galata see the threat to Constantinople and seem ready to indirectly support us, as they have grown concerned about the unchecked power of the Ottomans. However, Galata is not under our direct control, and officially it remains neutral, meaning that the Sultan most likely won’t attack it – at least not until he has dealt with us. How should we plan to use Galata in our siege plans?
-> Galata is crucial to our defence. We should try to bribe Angelo Lomellini, the Genoese podestà, mayor of the city. (7,500 gold) <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 7500 >>
    You extend an offer of alliance to Angelo Lomellini. A well-placed bribe persuades an ambitious podestà to covertly support your cause, ensuring Galata becomes an invaluable ally in your defence against the Ottoman threat.
    <<Change fGalataRep 0.3>> 
    <<Change iMoney -7500>>
-> Send the Varangian Guard to seize Galata by night. 65% success; lose 5–20 guards, 7 ships and 1 secret. Failure destroys Galata's goodwill. <<if GetInt("iVarangians") >= 50>>
    <<RndChange iVarangians -20 -5>>
    <<Change iShips -7>>
    <<Change iSecrets -1>>
    <<set $chance = dice(100)>>
    <<if $chance <= 65>>
        Before dawn, the Varangians seize the gates and the Tower of Christ. The harbour wakes beneath your banners. Genoese crews flee with their ships; possession has come at the price of trust.
        <<Change fGalataRep -0.1>>
        <<CaptureGalata varangians>>
        <<jump prepGalataGovernor>>
    <<else>>
        The alarm sounds before the gates are secured. Your surviving guards stagger back across the water. You disown the expedition: mutineers, you insist, acting without their Emperor's leave. The podestà accepts the words with a face like stone. He believes none of them.
        <<Change fGalataRep -1>>
    <<endif>>
-> Neutral, but friendly Galata will be most useful to us during the siege.
    You assure the podestà that his gates will remain his own. Galatan merchants continue to cross the harbour, and the captains exchange messages quietly. A friendly neighbour may prove more useful than a resentful subject.
    <<if GetInt("iGalataNeutralPledge") == 0>>
        <<SetInt iGalataNeutralPledge 1>>
        <<Change fGalataRep 0.1>>
    <<endif>> 
-> Allow Galata to remain neutral, but use your Imperial privilege to demand that they provide us with troops, supplies and money for the siege. <<if GetInt("iGalataSupportRequested") == 0>>
    You demand troops, provisions and money under your imperial privileges. The podestà sends what he can, protesting that you are dragging Galata into the war. The supplies arrive; the warmth of his letters does not.
    <<SetInt iGalataSupportRequested 1>>
    <<Change fGalataRep -0.2>>
    <<Change iInfantry 300>>
    <<Change iArchers 300>>
    <<Change iMoney 4500>>  
    <<Change iProvisions 500>> 
-> Send gifts to the people of Galata and remind them of their duty to Christianity, asking them to join you. <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 3 >> #focus:3
    You dispatch generous gifts to Galata, coupled with an impassioned plea reminding them of their Christian duty. Framing their involvement as a moral obligation to protect the faith from Ottoman dominance, you also hint at the fact that after Constantinople the next target for the ambitious Sultan will be Galata itself. While Genoese are men of coin first and faith second, your appeal does have some effect - you can expect more support from them now.
    <<Change fGalataRep 0.3>>
->	Ask Giustiniani to take control of Galata. His loyalty falls by 10; decide his reward after capture. <<if GetBool("bCharWarrior0") && GetInt("iFocus") == 4 >> #focus:4
    You order Giustiniani to secure Galata. The territorial settlement remains yours to decide. A Genoese captain will meet less resistance from its citizens than your own guards would. He understands the bargain perfectly. So do the men who will have to honour it.
    <<CaptureGalata giovanni>> 
    <<jump prepGalataGovernor>>
===
title: story16
---
<b>Siege Preparation — Assault on the Straitcutter Fortress</b>: <br> Rumeli fortress, conceived and built in 1452 on the orders of Sultan Mehmed II, was built in preparation for the planned siege, with the goal of cutting off maritime military and logistical relief that could potentially come to our aid by way of the Bosphorus Strait from the Black Sea. Hence the fortress's alternative name, Boğazkesen or "Strait-cutter". As the Ottoman armies are getting ready to besiege our City, we need to decide if we should use part of our resources to neutralise this fortress and open up the vital artery to the Black Sea ports, which could supply us with food required for the siege.
-> Order covert infiltration of the fortress by the Varangian Guard. {RumeliReconLabel()} <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Risk troops\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iVarangians") >= 10>>
    <<set $chance = dice(100)>>
    <<if $chance <= RumeliChance()>> 
    You task a group of your best warriors - Varangians - to slip into Rumeli Fortress under the cover of being merchants. Their mission is to start a fire and create confusion, creating an opening to open the gate and let in the rest of your forces. The mission goes flawlessly - the infiltrators skilfully navigate Ottoman defences and soon your troops raise your banner over the Boğazkesen. Your troops capture the enemy cannons which will be useful in the defence of our City and explode the gunpowder stores, turning the fortress into a ruin and opening the way to the Black Sea. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Straits are Open\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cannons +5\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Troop Morale +10\]</i></color>
    <<Change bStraitsOpen>> 
    <<Change fTroopRep 0.1>>
    <<Change iCannons 5>>
    <<else>>
    You task a group of your best warriors - Varangians - to slip into Rumeli Fortress under the cover of being merchants. Their mission is to start a fire and create confusion, creating an opening to open the gate and let in the rest of your forces.  Unfortunately Ottoman vigilance prevails as the infiltrators are detected. A swift and decisive response leads to their capture and execution. The rest of your troops return, seeing that there is no way to complete their mission. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Varangians -10\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Troop Morale -5\]</i></color>
    <<Change iVarangians -10>>
    <<Change fTroopRep -0.05>>
    <<endif>>
-> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Focus - Diplomacy and Spycraft\]</i></color> Bribe someone inside the castle to sabotage the Artillery <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cost - 3000 gold\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 4 && GetInt("iMoney") >= 3000 >> #focus:4
    <<Change iMoney -3000>>
    <<set $chance = dice(100)>>
    <<if $chance <= 85>> 
    There is no need to take over the whole fortress – the most important thing is to render it toothless by disabling the artillery. You order a sabotage mission and your spies bribe a Levantine sentry to set the gunpowder storage on fire. He is successful - the ammunition warehouse explodes with a thunderous roar, destroying the main artillery tower, destroying all the cannons and the ammunition required to fire it. The mission is a complete success and while Ottomans will eventually rebuild the castle, for now the Strait lies open once more. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Straits are Open\]</i></color>
    <<Change bStraitsOpen>> 
    <<else>>
    There is no need to take over the whole fortress – the most important thing is to render it toothless by disabling the artillery. You order a sabotage mission and your spies bribe a Levantine sentry to set the gunpowder storage on fire.  Unfortunately, Ottoman guards keenly guard the armoury and detect the sabotage attempt. Despite best efforts, the mission fails and our saboteur is executed. The artillery remains operational, maintaining the blockade of the Strait. Fortunately, the only thing we lost is a bit of money.  <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Money -3000\]</i></color>
    <<endif>>
-> Launch a sudden assault and take the castle at any cost. This will guarantee our victory, although at a great cost. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[High casualties\]</i></color>
    You realise that taking this fortress is critical for the survival of your City. The sudden assault catches Rumeli’s defenders off guard, and the well-equipped contingent led by the seasoned commander overwhelms the defences. The fortress falls under your control, securing the Bosphorus Strait, but the price for this is great, as direct assault is always costly. 
    <<Change iInfantry -300>>
    <<Change iArchers -50>>
    <<Change bStraitsOpen>>  
-> Undermine the foundations -  send your engineers to put explosives underneath the fortress <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Risk - Engineers & Ammunition\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iAmmunition") >= 15 && GetInt("iEngineers") >= 5 >>
    <<set $chance = dice(100)>>
    <<if $chance <= 90>> 
    You deploy your engineers to dig tunnels beneath Rumeli Fortress, intending to collapse its walls by igniting explosive charges. Your engineers complete the tunnel and successfully detonate the charges, causing the fortress walls to collapse. The fortress is now barely more than a smoking ruin, leaving the Strait open.
    <<Change iAmmunition -15>>
    <<Change bStraitsOpen>> 
    <<else>>
    <<Change iEngineers -5>> 
    <<Change iAmmunition -15>>
    You deploy your engineers to dig tunnels beneath Rumeli Fortress, intending to collapse its walls by igniting explosive charges. Unfortunately keen-eyed Ottoman scouts discover the tunnels and collapse them before your sappers reach their destination, trapping and killing your engineers. It is a black day, we will not have access to the Black Sea.
    <<endif>>
-> Conserve our strength and do nothing, we do not need access to the Black Sea.
    Boğazkesen is too well fortified and we need all the men we can spare in Constantinople. However, this means that we will not have access to the Black Sea ports and their grain reserves.
===
title: story17
---
<b>Siege Preparation — Greek Fire</b> <br> As the looming threat of the Ottoman siege casts its shadow over Constantinople, the question arises – should the Empire embark on the quest to rediscover the ancient knowledge of Greek Fire and prepare to use it during the siege? This legendary incendiary weapon, a closely guarded state secret, could potentially tip the scales in the impending conflict. It was known for its exceptional effectiveness in setting targets ablaze, even on water. Greek Fire was typically deployed using specialised siphon-like devices mounted on ships, allowing the Byzantines to unleash devastating fire upon their enemies. The weapon played a significant role in the defence of Constantinople and maybe it could do so again. However, it has been centuries since the last time it was used in battle. Although we have some clues, rediscovering the secret will be a complicated and expensive process. Shall we try to embark on it?
-> Have the scholars search the Blachernae archives for recipes and accounts of Greek Fire. <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cost - 4000 gold\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 4000 >>
    <<set $chance = dice(100)>>
    <<if $chance <= 75>>  
    Scholars meticulously comb through the historical archives, unearthing fragments of information on Greek Fire. While the process is slow, their dedicated efforts yield valuable insights, providing a foundation for the rediscovery of the Greek Fire! We now have a powerful weapon in our hands against the Turks!
    <<Change bTechGreekFire TRUE>>
    <<Change iMoney -4000>>
    <<else>>
    Scholars meticulously comb through the historical archives, trying to unearth fragments of information on Greek Fire. They discover many interesting secrets, but unfortunately the mystery of the Greek Fire appears to have been lost in the mists of time and cannot be found.
    <<Change iMoney -4000>>
    <<Change iSecrets 1>>
    <<endif>>
-> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Focus - Faith and Unity\]</i></color> Send an expedition to Mount Athos monasteries, where ancient manuscripts and forgotten wisdom are hidden. The monks most likely know the secret of the Greek Fire. <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 3 >> #focus:3
    A daring expedition sets forth to the sacred Mount Athos, seeking the wisdom preserved by devout monks. The journey proves fruitful, as the monks, bound by faith and a sense of duty, share ancient manuscripts that hold the elusive formula for Greek Fire. We now have a powerful weapon in our hands against the Turks!
    <<Change bTechGreekFire TRUE>>
-> Summon all the alchemists to test new methods to create Greek fire – invention beats rediscovery! <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Cost - 3000 gold\]</i></color> <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 3000 >>
    <<Change iMoney -3000>>
    <<set $chance = dice(100)>>
    <<if $chance <= 30>>  
    Through an unexpected stroke of genius, or perhaps sheer luck, the alchemists rediscover the ancient formula for Greek Fire. After months of trial and error, they successfully create a working version of the legendary weapon, one that burns fiercely and even floats on water. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Technology Unlocked - Greek Fire\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Money -3000\]</i></color>
    <<Change bTechGreekFire TRUE>>
    <<elseif $chance <= 60>>
    While the alchemists cannot recreate Greek Fire, their experiments with explosive substances lead to an unexpected breakthrough - the creation of small, throwable devices that explode upon impact. These rudimentary hand grenades are not as destructive as Greek Fire but can be used as an effective weapon in close combat situations. We now have unlocked a new technology - Grenades! <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Technology Unlocked - Grenades\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Money -3000\]</i></color>
    <<Change bTechGrenades TRUE>>
    <<elseif $chance <= 90>>
    Despite the best efforts of the alchemists, the process of recreating Greek Fire ends in failure. Their attempts at various combinations of chemicals and alchemical processes result in only smoke and minor explosions. Nothing close to the feared weapon is produced and your troops and nobles are disappointed. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Noble Support -3\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Troop Morale -3\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Money -3000\]</i></color>
    <<else>>
    The alchemists' reckless experiments take a disastrous turn. A chemical reaction goes horribly wrong, causing an explosion that destroys part of the palace. The explosion spreads fires across the lower levels, and several buildings are damaged - we will need to cover the costs of their reconstruction. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Money -1000\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Money -3000\]</i></color> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Materials -25\]</i></color>
    <<Change iMoney -1000>>
    <<Change iMaterials -25>>
    <<endif>>
-> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Focus - Fortifications and Artillery\]</i></color> We should not rely on legends for our defence. Battles of this modern age will be decided by steel and cannon, not some esoteric alchemy. (3,000 gold) <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 0 && GetInt("iMoney") >= 3000 >> #focus:0
    <<Change iMoney -3000>>
    You put the engineers to work on steel and powder. They choose firing positions along the walls and train the gunners to aim at the Ottoman batteries. Our cannon will answer theirs. Counter-battery fire is now available.
    <<Change bTechCounterCannons TRUE>>
-> We have other matters to deal with, leave these fairytales for someone else.
    We decided that Greek Fire is not necessary for our plans on how to defend the City during the siege. Time will show if our decision was wise.
===
title: story18
---
<b>Siege Preparation — Orders for the Fleet</b>: <br> It is clear that the Empire is too weak to meet Mehmed in the open field, however our small, but professional fleet is one of the few tools we have which we could still use offensively against our enemy. Our fleet is currently {GetInt("iShips")}, while the Ottoman western fleet gathered for the siege by your estimation has around 31 galleys and a large number of rowboats. Our admirals think that victory in a direct battle is possible, but far from guaranteed. Some suggest that we should instead use the fleet in a more defensive manner. What shall be the purpose of our fleet during the siege? We will be able to change this mission at a later point, however we should consider carefully.
{NavalReport()}
-> The fleet should be used to raid Ottoman food shipping and disrupt their logistics
    Our fleet will now engage in raiding activities, interfering with Turkish supply routes, causing Ottoman food supplies to run out quicker than they would otherwise.
    <<PrepFleet 0>>
-> The fleet should be ordered to raid Ottoman coastal villages and target valuable merchant ships, we could use their plunder to fund the siege.
    Your fleet will now engage in a guerrilla campaign against the soft underbelly of the Ottoman Empire, attacking unprotected villages and trade ships, seeking to cause chaos and plunder gold to bring it back to you.
    <<PrepFleet 1>>
-> As Rumeli fortress is no longer a threat, our fleet should establish a lifeline to the Genoese colonies in Crimea and bring us grain. <<if GetBool("bStraitsOpen")>>
    On your orders the fleet will travel to Genoese colonies in Crimea, we know that their markets are full of cheap grain - they will run the blockade and keep bringing supplies to the city.
    <<PrepFleet 2>>
-> Order the fleet to remain in the Golden Horn, protecting the city and shooting their cannons at the attackers.
    <<PrepFleet 3>>
-> Order the fleet to visit various ports around the Mediterranean Sea and bring volunteers, supplies and donations to help the besieged City.
    <<PrepFleet 4>>
-> Order the fleet to engage the Ottoman fleet in direct battle and destroy them. 
    <<set $chance = dice(100)>>
    <<if $chance <= NavalChance()>> 
    Your fleet finds the Ottoman ships off Lesbos and forces them into battle. By nightfall, the surviving enemy vessels are fleeing. News of the victory reaches the City ahead of the returning captains.
    <<NavalBattle true>>
    <<jump story18a>>
    <<else>>
    <<jump story18b>>
    <<endif>>
===
title: story18b
---
<b>Defeated Fleet</b>: <br> The Ottoman ships hold the sea. Our fleet is defeated, its mission abandoned. Battered survivors return to the Golden Horn, towing what damaged hulls they can save. Other crews will never return. The remaining ships take up the defence of the harbour; future orders can be given through the Fleet menu.
<<NavalBattle false>>
{NavalReport()}
===

title: story18a
---
<b>Victorious Fleet</b>: <br> Now that our fleet has claimed an amazing victory over the Turks at Lesbos, it has much more freedom to operate and requires new orders. Enemy survivors remain at sea, and new ships will reinforce them. Our captains can seek new opportunities along the sea routes. Give the victorious fleet its next mission. You may later change it in the core Fleet menu by spending an action. 
{NavalReport()}
-> The fleet should be used to raid Ottoman food shipping and disrupt their logistics, so that their food supplies would run out as soon as possible.
    <<SetInt iFleet 0>>
-> The fleet should be ordered to raid Ottoman coastal villages and target valuable merchant ships, we could use their plunder to fund the siege.
    <<SetInt iFleet 1>>
-> The fleet should travel to the Genoese colonies in Crimea, we know that their markets are full of cheap grain - they should run the blockade and keep bringing supplies to the city whenever possible.
    <<SetInt iFleet 2>>
-> Order the fleet to remain in the Golden Horn, protecting the city and shooting their cannons at the attackers.
    <<SetInt iFleet 3>>
-> Order the fleet to visit various ports around the Mediterranean Sea and bring volunteers, supplies and donations to help the besieged City.
    <<SetInt iFleet 4>>
===
title: story19
---
<b>Siege Preparation — The Great Chain of the Golden Horn</b> <br> One of the most unique defence mechanisms of our great City is the famous Great Chain of the Golden Horn. It was initially constructed as a response to naval threats from hostile forces during times of siege and first proved itself during the Arab assaults on our city in the 8th century of our Lord. Composed of massive interlocking iron links, the chain spans the width of the Golden Horn, reaching around 750 metres in length, effectively blocking enemy ships from entering the harbour and providing an additional layer of defence to the city. However, some of our advisers claim that the Chain has outlived its usefulness and could be repurposed, either sold as scrap metal to raise funds for the siege preparations or melted to cast cannons and ammunition, to strengthen our artillery.
-> They are fools! Without the Chain, Golden Horn will be open to enemy fleet assaults and we could lose access to Galata! We must keep the Chain.
    Your decision to retain the Great Chain proves crucial. As enemy fleets attempt to navigate the Golden Horn, they will be met with the formidable obstacle to their plans. The Chain holds strong, safeguarding the harbour and preventing potential assaults and our strategic access to Galata is preserved.
-> They are right, the Chain serves no purpose in modern times. Scrap it and sell the metal.
    Workers dismantle the Chain and sell the metal. The treasury receives the proceeds; the harbour entrance must now be defended by ships and shore batteries.
    <<Change iMoney 10000>>
    <<Change bGoldenHornChain FALSE>>
-> We shall defend our harbour with our cannons, get rid of the Chain and use the metal to cast ammunition and cannons. However, it will cost 5000 gold. <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 5000>>
    Foundry workers cut up the Chain and recast the metal into cannon and ammunition. The work consumes gold, but new guns can now cover the harbour.
    <<Change iAmmunition 100>>
    <<Change iCannons 10>>
    <<Change iMoney -5000>>
-> The Chain is absolutely massive… We could ask Orban to cast the greatest cannon in the world from its metal. (5,000 gold) <<if GetBool("bCharArtillerist5") && GetInt("iMoney") >= 5000>> 
    <<Change iMoney -5000>>
    Orban has the Chain dragged to the foundry. The furnaces burn through the night, and the mould is packed beneath a forest of wooden braces. At last the Grand Cannon emerges. The gunfounders cheer; the treasurer asks whether anyone has weighed how much powder it will need.
    <<SetInt iGrandCannon 2>>
    <<Change bGoldenHornChain 0>>
===
title: story20
---
<b>Fate of Stadia and Therapia Castles</b>: <br> Stadia and Therapia guard approaches to Constantinople, but their garrisons are too weak to withstand the Ottoman army for long. Recall their defenders, sacrifice the outposts to gain time, reinforce their resistance, or hand them to the Genoese. <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Delays postpone enemy operations during the siege; the calendar and the survival count continue.\]</i></color>
-> Withdraw the garrisons. Gain 100 archers.
    <<Change iArchers 100>>
    The garrisons withdraw into the City. One hundred archers join your defenders; the abandoned castles will not delay the enemy.
-> Leave the garrisons to delay the Ottomans. Delay enemy operations for 5–9 days; troop morale −5.
    <<RndChange iEnemyDelay 5 9>>
    <<Change fTroopRep -0.05>>
    The garrisons remain at their posts. Their resistance buys five to nine days without Ottoman operations against the City. Their sacrifice will not bring reinforcements home.
-> Reinforce the castles. Send 200 infantry and 100 materials; gain 9 days of delay. <<if GetInt("iInfantry") >= 200 && GetInt("iMaterials") >= 100>>
    <<Change iInfantry -200>>
    <<Change iMaterials -100>>
    <<Change iEnemyDelay 9>>
    Two hundred infantry and a convoy of materials leave the City. Their resistance will occupy the Ottoman forces for nine days. These troops are committed to the outposts and will not return to the City's garrison.
-> Grant the castles to the Genoese. Gain 100 archers and 15 points of Galata relations.
    <<Change iArchers 100>>
    <<Change fGalataRep 0.15>>
    <<SetInt iGalataCastles 1>>
    <<Change iGalataClaims 1>>
    The garrisons return and the podestà accepts the gift. Galata's goodwill improves, but neutrality leaves the Ottoman advance unimpeded.
===
title: story21
---
<b>The Sultan's Envoy Arrives</b>: <br> Beneath a white pennant, a rider approaches the gate. The guns fall silent long enough for the iron shoes of his horse to be heard upon the stones. In the audience chamber he bows with exquisite care, as though already measuring the distance between a sovereign and a subject. <br> The Sultan offers peace, he says. Beyond the windows lie the roofs, gardens and church domes of a thousand years; beyond the walls, the army that would inherit them. The sealed letter rests between you. Your answer will travel farther than this room.
-> Refuse publicly: +5 troop morale points, +10 Sultan fury points.
    <<Change fTroopRep 0.05>>
    <<Change fSultanRage 0.1>>
    Your refusal is read aloud at the gates. Your soldiers take heart; the envoy leaves in anger.
-> Receive him courteously and question him before declining: gain 1 secret.
    <<Change iSecrets 1>>
    Your household prolongs the audience and compares the envoy's claims with the reports of your scouts. You gain a useful piece of intelligence without making a promise of surrender.
-> Execute the envoy. Sultan's fury +20 points; noble support −5 points.
    You order the envoy taken from the chamber. His letter remains unopened upon the table. By evening his riderless horse is sent through the gate; by dawn the Ottoman drums answer. Even those who praised your defiance find little to say of this.
    <<Change fSultanRage 0.2>>
    <<Change fNobleRep -0.05>>

===
title: story22
---
<b>Easter Speech</b>: <br> As you gaze out over the majestic City below you, you contemplate the dire situation facing your beloved Empire. With the mighty Ottoman forces encircling the city, you realise that siege will begin in mere hours, a couple of days at most. Heavy responsibility lies on your shoulders. While everyone knows about the impending siege, you are on your way to Hagia Sophia to rally the spirits of your people for the impending battle. Everyone will be there, from the lowest whores to the most esteemed nobles. What will you focus on in your speech?
-> Citizens of the Roman Empire! We are the defenders of a legacy that spans millennia. Let every heart beat as one, for it is our resilience and unwavering spirit...
    Your speech leaves a generally good impression on everyone gathered at the Hagia Sophia, but the nobles and the general population love it more than the rest.
    <<Change fPeopleRep 0.15>>
    <<Change fNobleRep 0.15>> 
    <<Change fTroopRep 0.05>> 
    <<Change fPriestRep 0.05>>  
-> Soldiers of Christ! We have gathered here under the majestic dome of our blessed temple, to worship our God and to steel ourselves to defend our creed...
    Your speech leaves a generally good impression on everyone gathered at the Hagia Sophia, but the troops and the priests love it more than the rest.
    <<Change fPeopleRep 0.05>>
    <<Change fNobleRep 0.05>> 
    <<Change fTroopRep 0.15>> 
    <<Change fPriestRep 0.15>>
-> My faithful subjects, all who live and breathe to serve our great cause - it is time for us to prepare to meet our enemies...
    Your speech leaves a generally good impression on everyone gathered at the Hagia Sophia.
    <<Change fPeopleRep 0.1>>
    <<Change fNobleRep 0.1>> 
    <<Change fTroopRep 0.1>> 
    <<Change fPriestRep 0.1>>
-> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[Focus - War Economy\]</i></color> Order the Varangian Guard to close the doors and make everyone pay a war tax before they leave. Make exception for the priests if they help you convince the rest. <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 2 >> #focus:2
    People feel somewhat betrayed, but there's little they can do and the priests are happy to preach to them about the joys of donating for the common cause. Some nobles have to leave their jewellery, while the poor end up leaving their last pennies. But you know that you need this money more than they do.
    <<Change fPeopleRep -0.1>>
    <<Change fNobleRep -0.1>> 
    <<Change fTroopRep -0.1>> 
    <<Change fPriestRep 0.1>>
    <<Change iMoney 7500>>
-> It is not for the speeches that we have gathered here! Let everyone feast and enjoy themselves for one last time before the siege begins! Let us celebrate, for tomorrow we may die!
    On your orders, the guards roll in barrels of wine and start handing out bread, while musicians strike up uplifting tunes. The priests are unhappy about their temple becoming a feast hall, but they cannot do anything. People who moments ago were sullen and concerned, soon relax, drink and laugh. You can only hope that this last proper feast will help them withstand the coming months of trials and deprivation.
    <<Change iProvisions {-(FoodNeed()*3)}>>
    <<Change iMoney -2500>>
    <<Change fPeopleRep 0.2>>
    <<Change fNobleRep 0.2>> 
    <<Change fTroopRep 0.2>> 
    <<Change fPriestRep -0.05>>
===
title: story23
---
<b>Siege Begins</b>: <br> So the time has come. You can see Mehmed's army marching in the distance, sky in their direction is marked by plumes of black smoke from the villages they set on fire while coming here. The siege of Constantinople has begun. <br> <br> <color=\#B8CFDA><i>\[You have now entered siege phase. Please note that while in the early game turns marked passing weeks, currently each turn marks a passing day. Furthermore, as food will now stop coming into the city, your provisions will start decreasing at a rate based on your population size. Enemy will be attacking your walls daily and when they will be reduced enough, they will launch an assault, trying to take your head. Good luck.\]</i></color> 
-> Greet our guests - send the cavalry to harass their forward formations, but avoid serious engagements.
    <<set $chance = dice(100)>>
    <<if $chance <= (GetInt("iCavalry")/10+10)>> 
    Your cavalry, adept at hit-and-run tactics, successfully disrupts the Ottoman vanguard, overrunning a surprised column of slaves, engineers and cooks and inflicting notable damage without being drawn into a decisive battle. One of your knights even manages to set a gunpowder wagon on fire, exploding it and destroying a few enemy cannons. The manoeuvre not only sets the stage for a more fortified defence but also instills a renewed sense of confidence among your ranks.
    <<Change iTurkCannons -5>> 
    <<RndChange iBazouks -517 -1578>> 
    <<RndChange iCavalry -5 -25>>
    <<else>>
    Your decision to send the cavalry to harass Ottoman forward formations takes a disastrous turn. The seemingly straightforward plan unravels as the Turks send their light steppe horsemen to intercept your force. They are more formidable than expected and manage to repel your cavalry. What was intended as a show of strength turns into a minor defeat as both sides suffer casualties.
    <<RndChange iBazouks -312 -895>> 
    <<RndChange iCavalry -25 -50>>
    <<endif>>
-> It is time for the Imperator to stretch his legs. I am going to personally lead the cavalry charge. Our goal is to kill as many of them as possible and retreat.
    <<set $chance = dice(100)>>
    <<if $chance <= (GetInt("iCavalry")/10+20)>> 
    Your charge catches the Ottoman vanguard before it can form. The horsemen break through, then turn for the gates. Several saddles return empty; hundreds of enemies lie on the road.
    <<RndChange iBazouks -751 -2565>> 
    <<RndChange iCavalry -5 -25>>
    <<elseif $chance <= (GetInt("iCavalry")/10+45)>> 
    Your charge catches the Ottoman vanguard before it can form. The horsemen break through, then turn for the gates. Several saddles return empty; hundreds of enemies lie on the road.
    <<RndChange iBazouks -312 -895>>
    <<RndChange iCavalry -50 -100>>
    <<else>>
    Your charge catches the Ottoman vanguard before it can form. The horsemen break through, then turn for the gates. Several saddles return empty; hundreds of enemies lie on the road.
    <<SetInt iCourtState5 3>>
    <<ChangeFraction fHealth -1>>
    <<endif>>
-> Conserve our forces. Set the fields and houses in front of the city on fire. // 
    Ashes and dust will be all that will greet our invaders. Let them feast on that! We must hope that they will not be able to procure enough food to sustain this siege for long...
    <<Change iFood -20>> 
-> Infiltrate the Ottoman camp. Seek informants among its interpreters, drivers and camp followers; the strength of the network remains uncertain. \[Cost: 5,000 gold\] <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 5000>>
    <<set $openingAgents = OpeningRecruits()>>
    Quiet approaches to interpreters, drivers and camp followers establish {$openingAgents} new agents inside the Ottoman camp. Their reports will begin reaching your spymaster.
    <<Change iSpies {$openingAgents}>>
    <<Change iMoney -5000>> 
===
title: story24
---
<b>Ottomans use prisoners to fill the Moat </b> <br> The moat protecting the Theodosian Walls is a serious issue for the Ottoman war machine and they have to deal with it before they can hope to breach the walls themselves. <br>Unfortunately, Ottomans have managed to capture enough prisoners, primarily refugees from the countryside, some slaves and soldiers captured in the skirmishes to form a labour detachment a few thousand strong, which they are sending now towards the walls. <br> Ottoman state is run with a whip and brutal discipline – even their elite Janissaries are slave-soldiers, so there is no mercy to be expected, their plan is clear. Turks are herding the prisoners towards the moat and will force them to fill it with earth or their own bodies, both options acceptable. <br> You hear your soldiers murmuring atop the walls – the archers anxiously play with arrows in their quivers, waiting for your orders. It is one thing to shoot at the invaders, but entirely another to shoot at your own countrymen, taken prisoner and forced to labour on the enemy’s behalf. Your siege commander turns to you. <br> “Emperor, we cannot let the moat be filled. God forgive us, but we need to order our archers to fire on the enslaved labourers, otherwise we shall all soon be slaves. What are your orders?”
-> Order archers to fire on our enslaved people.
    Your command echoes across the walls, and reluctantly, your archers draw their bows. The first volleys rain down upon the enslaved prisoners, a brutal decision made in the face of strategic necessity. The moat is protected, but the atmosphere atop the walls turns dark. The morale of your troops falls, but you try to convince yourself that this sacrifice was necessary.
    <<Change fTroopRep -0.15>> 
-> We don’t need the moat. Fire only at the Turkish guards, but spare the labourers.
    You prioritise preserving the moral integrity of your defenders. The order is given to focus solely on the Ottoman forces, sparing the enslaved labourers. While the moat remains a formidable obstacle, your archers concentrate their fire on the advancing Turks. The labourers fill up the moat, but some of them use the opportunity to run over to the walls, your soldiers throw them down ropes and help them get over the walls.
    <<Change fTroopRep 0.05>>
    <<RndChange iBazouks -200 -800>>
    <<Change fMoat -0.5>>
-> Open the gates: rescue 1,000–3,000 people, gain 5 troop morale points, lose 30 moat integrity points.
    The gates open under covering fire. Prisoners run towards the City while Ottoman archers shoot into the fleeing crowd. You bring survivors inside, but enemy labourers fill another stretch of the moat. Moat integrity -30 points.
    <<Change fTroopRep 0.05>>
    <<RndChange iPopulation 1000 3000>>
    <<Change fMoat -0.3>>
-> Emperor cannot abandon his people. Sally out!
    <<set $chance = dice(100)>>
    <<if $chance <= 50>>
    The cavalry drives the guards away from the prisoners. The captives scatter towards the gates, and the Ottoman labourers abandon their work at the moat.
    <<elseif $chance <= 80>>
    The cavalry drives the guards away from the prisoners. The captives scatter towards the gates, and the Ottoman labourers abandon their work at the moat.
    <<RndChange iCavalry -30 -90>>
    <<Change fMoat -0.3>>
    <<else>>
    The cavalry drives the guards away from the prisoners. The captives scatter towards the gates, and the Ottoman labourers abandon their work at the moat.
    <<RndChange iCavalry -30 -90>>
    <<RndChange fHealth -0.1 -0.5>>
    <<Change fMoat -0.3>>
    <<endif>>
===


title: quietDay
---
No new petition reaches the council today. Your standing preparations will now face the next enemy action.
===
